Hello,

I conducted a line transect survey in a tropical peat swamp forest to
estimate the density and abundance of the 4 endemic primate species, and =
I
would be happy for some advice concerning the evaluation of the data:

The transect system which was established consisted of 10 individual
transects of equal length (1,2 km), each branching off in a 90 degree ang=
le
from a main access path. Five transects (01, 03, 05, 07, 09) branch off t=
o
the East and five (02, 04, 06, 08, 10) branch off to the west in an
alternating fashion. Each transect on each side of the access path is spa=
ced
400 m apart and the starting point of the whole system was chosen randoml=
y.

Because of the variable weather conditions (lots of rain) and the limited=

study period, it was important to achieve a survey effort which is high
enough to allow a minimum number observations. Therefore, a temporal syst=
em
with three survey time slots per day was used (A) 7. =96 9.00, B) 9.30 =96=

11.30, C) 15.30 =96 17.30) =96 according to the main activity pattern of =
local
primates, derived from literature as well as talks with locals). Througho=
ut
the study period, each transect was surveyed three to five times (combini=
ng
the slots A, B and C). While it was envisaged to walk EACH transect 5 tim=
es,
this was not possible due to heavy earthquakes and rainfall.=20

My questions:

How should I treat the data for evaluation? Is it a huge problem (&#8=
594; bias)
that some transects were walked 3 and others up to 5 times? Can I pool th=
e
observations made within the different time slots for each transect (taki=
ng
into consideration that it was never possible to actually conduct 3 surve=
ys
per day)?!

Also, for two species between 80 to 100 observations were obtained, but f=
or
the other two only 17 and a lot of these observations were only acoustic
encounters. In fact, for three species, the majority of the observations
were acoustic ones (81, 15, 14). How can I ensure a proper determination =
of
the cluster sizes for these animals? Should I just take the mean cluster
size of all the groups that were clearly visible?

For the two species with only 17 observations (14 acoustic & 3 visual=
,
respectively), is there a way to produce reliable estimates with distance=

(considering that a minimum of forty observations should normally be achi=
eved)?

Any help with this is greatly appreciated!!!

Thank you

Marcel

