David, that would give you estimates of abundance within the circular=20
plot of radius 50m, which of course can easily be converted to density=20
estimates.  It assumes that for the same covariates, detectability was=20
the same whether or not distances were recorded.

Using the mcds engine, species can go in as a factor, allowing you to=20
combine data across species, which allows analysis of rarer species.  See=
:

Alldredge, M. W., Pollock, K. H., Simons, T. R. and Shriner, S. A.=20
(2007) Multiple-species analysis of point count data: a more=20
parsimonious modelling framework. /J. App. Ecol/. 44: 281-290.

Marques, T.A., Thomas, L., Fancy, S.G. and Buckland, S.T. 2007. =20
Improving estimates of bird density using multiple covariate distance=20
sampling.  /The Auk/ *124*, 1229-1243.

Steve Buckland

